wn, White, 2Step=F (since T1? Wait, no. Wait the 2Step is either F or T, and Moved is 0 or 1.\n\nWait the description says:\n\n* 2Step (En Passant flag)\n    * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n    * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n* Moved (Movement flag)\n    * `1` - Has moved before\n    * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n\nSo for the White pawn on h4: it's P W T1. Wait, no. Wait the example given: PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant (2Step=F), already moved (Moved=1). So the third character is 2Step (F/T), fourth is Moved (0/1).\n\nWait the example says PWF1: White Pawn, 2Step=F (so didn't move two squares last turn), Moved=1 (has moved before).\n\nBut in the current board, the White pawn on h4 is shown as PWT1. Wait, looking back at the board:\n\nIn row 4, h4 has PWT1. So the code is P W T 1? Wait no, the encoding is [Type][Color][2Step][Moved]. So for h4, it's P (pawn), W (white), T (2Step=T, meaning it moved two squares last turn), and 1 (has moved before). Wait but Moved=1 means it has moved before, which makes sense because it's on h4. But 2Step=T would mean that it moved two squares in its last move, which would make it eligible for en passant. But since it's on h4, the last move must have been from h2 to h4 (two squares), so yes, 2Step=T. But in the board representation, it's written as PWT1. Wait, the third character is T (2Step=T), fourth is 1 (Moved=1). So that pawn is en passant eligible.\n\nBut in the current turn, it's Black's move. So Black can capture en passant if they have a pawn adjacent to h4. Looking at the board, Black's pawns are all on rank 7. Wait, no, Black's pawns are on rank 7 (row 7), which is correct. But for en passant, the capturing pawn needs to be on the same rank as the target square. Wait, en passant is when a pawn moves two squares from its starting position, and an enemy pawn can capture it as if it had moved one square. So if White's pawn moved from h2 to h4, then Black's pawn on g7 or h7 could